    "attributes": {
        "action": "Review assessment of young people under the Mental Capacity Act",
        "additional_details": "The brain keeps developing into the mid-20s, affecting impulse control and risk judgement, yet the MCA assumes one adult standard from age 16. Assessments in acute crisis can capture a person at their worst, not their usual capacity. We believe the \"unwise decision\" principle can leave at-risk young people without help until it is too late. Around 1 in 5 young people aged 16-24 has a mental health condition (Centre for Mental Health, 2020). We believe reform is needed now.",
        "background": "Many Mental Capacity Act (MCA) capacity assessments still use what we see as outdated, blanket methods that fail 16-25 year-olds, even in crisis. We urge new age-specific guidance and a review of assessments during crisis, so vulnerable young people get support, not blame.",
